# Working memory protocol

Chat does not persist. These rules are the project's memory. Treat them as the source of truth across sessions.

## Read

Before non-trivial work, read `.cursor/rules/memory.mdc`. Pull in `@decisions.mdc`, `@conventions.mdc`, or `@lessons.mdc` when the task touches those topics.

## Write

When you learn something that should still be true next week, persist it in the same turn:

| You learned | Update |
| --- | --- |
| Product, stack, status, env var **names**, enabled MCP, current focus | `memory.mdc` |
| An architecture or tool choice and why | `decisions.mdc` |
| A coding or repo convention | `conventions.mdc` |
| A failure mode and the fix | `lessons.mdc` |
| A multi-step workflow | a skill, not a rule |

Use the `update-working-memory` skill. Keep `memory.mdc` under ~80 lines. Prefer bullets. No secrets. No transcripts.

## Do not

- Do not rely on prior chat to remember decisions.
- Do not duplicate the same fact in every rule.
- Do not put procedures in always-apply rules.
- Do not leave `Status` as `unbootstrapped` after you have set the project up.
